<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drogo of Hauteville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">?–<a href="/years/1051">1051</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Drogo of Hauteville was a Norman adventurer who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> as count from 1046 until his murder in 1051. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred of Hauteville</a>, he came to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iron-arm">William Iron Arm</a> and succeeded him, receiving investiture from Emperor Henry III in 1047. He was assassinated in a church at Montolio, probably in a conspiracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> nobles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drogo-of-hauteville.jpg" alt="Drogo of Hauteville" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giogo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_cath%C3%A9drale_Coutances_Drogon_de_Hauteville.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Drogo was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred of Hauteville</a> by his first wife Muriella. Goffredo Malaterra makes him the second son, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iron-arm">William Iron Arm</a>, while Romuald Guarna puts him third, after Serlo and Geoffrey. As Tancred's lands could not support all his sons, Drogo went to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> around 1035 with William and their brother Humphrey to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainulf-drengot">Rainulf Drengot</a>, Count of Aversa, and fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaimar-iv-of-salerno">Guaimar IV of Salerno</a> against Pandulf IV, Prince of Capua.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Guaimar's service the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> forces under Arduin joined the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maniakes">George Maniakes</a> in his attempt to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from the Saracens, and Drogo fought at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> and the reconquest of Syracuse. After a quarrel over spoils they left, and in 1040 joined a Lombard rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. When William was made Count of all the Normans in Apulia in September 1042, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a>, Drogo received Venosa, and in 1045 he occupied Bovino.</p><p>Drogo won the succession after a contest with Peter I of Trani, and Guaimar gave him his daughter Gaitelgrima in marriage. He paid homage to Emperor Henry III on 3 February 1047 and was invested as Count of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, while Henry stripped Guaimar of the title Duke of Apulia. Drogo received his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> coldly, sent him to Calabria without a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> and at first opposed his marriage to Alberada of Buonalbergo. He also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-capua">Richard Drengot</a>, a claimant to Aversa, prisoner for a time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Pope Leo IX</a>, Drogo was killed with several followers on Saint Lawrence's Day, 10 August 1051, as he entered the chapel of his castle to hear mass. Goffredo Malaterra records that the killer was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> man of his entourage. He was buried at the Abbey of Santissima Trinità of Venosa and succeeded by his brother Humphrey. With his first wife, Altrude, he had three children, Richard, Rocca and Eremburga.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Drogo of Hauteville” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his brothers <b>Drogo</b> and Humphrey, their half-brother Robert Guiscard took over in 1057, and in 1059 Pope Nicholas II invested him at Melfi as duke of Apulia, Calabria and, in prospect, Sicily, making the papacy the duchy's overlord.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria">Duchy of Apulia and Calabria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotentin-peninsula">Cotentin Peninsula</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Orsara di Puglia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.5,-1.5&amp;year=1051" aria-label="Cotentin Peninsula on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Drogo of Hauteville"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4917 5892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4917 5892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.5,-1.5&amp;year=1051">What was here in 1051? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
